  "guidebook": {
    "intro": "Every tense has a negative, and most start with h- or si-. Learn to turn down a cup of tea politely, and to say what didn’t happen.",
    "phrases": [
      { "target": "Sitaki chai, asante.", "base": "I don’t want tea, thanks." },
      { "target": "Si kweli!", "base": "It’s not true!" },
      { "target": "Mimi si mwalimu.", "base": "I’m not a teacher." },
      { "target": "Bado.", "base": "Not yet." },
      { "target": "Labda kesho.", "base": "Maybe tomorrow." },
      { "target": "Jana sikuenda shuleni.", "base": "Yesterday I didn’t go to school." }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "negatives",
        "title": "Saying “don’t”",
        "body": [
          "The negative of the present drops **-na-**, puts a negative prefix in front and ends the verb in **-i**: **ninataka** (I want) becomes **sitaki** (I don’t want).",
          "The prefixes are **si-** (I), **hu-** (you), **ha-** (he or she), **hatu-** (we), **ham-** (you all) and **hawa-** (they)."
        ],
        "table": [
          ["Want", "Don’t want"],
          ["ninataka", "sitaki"],
          ["unataka", "hutaki"],
          ["anataka", "hataki"],
          ["tunataka", "hatutaki"],
          ["wanataka", "hawataki"]
        ],
        "targetColumns": [0, 1]
      },
      {
        "id": "si-not",
        "title": "“Is not”: si",
        "body": [
          "**Ni** (is, am, are) has a short negative: **si**. **Mimi si mwalimu** (I’m not a teacher), **Si kweli** (it’s not true), **Si mbaya** (not bad).",
          "**Bado** on its own answers “not yet”. **Labda** (maybe) and **hata kidogo** (not at all) soften or strengthen an answer."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "past-future-negative",
        "title": "Didn’t and won’t",
        "body": [
          "For the past, the negative uses **-ku-** instead of **-li-**: **nilienda** (I went) becomes **sikuenda** (I didn’t go), **nilikula** becomes **sikula**.",
          "For the future, keep **-ta-** and use the negative prefix: **nitaenda** (I will go) becomes **sitaenda** (I won’t go), **hatutaenda** (we won’t go)."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Saying no softly",
        "body": [
          "A flat **hapana** can sound abrupt to a host. People often decline with **Asante** and a smile, or put it off with **labda** (maybe) or **nitaona** (I’ll see). Hosts may offer three times, so a gentle refusal is expected."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
